AZ-104 Lab 02a: Manage Subscriptions and RBAC
Overview

In this lab, I learned how to manage Azure subscriptions and implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) within Microsoft Azure. The lab focused on organizing subscriptions using Management Groups, assigning built-in Azure roles, creating custom RBAC roles, and monitoring permission changes using Activity Logs.

The objective was to securely manage access to Azure resources while following the principle of least privilege.

Task 1: Implement Management Groups

The first task involved creating and configuring Azure Management Groups.

Management Groups are used to organize Azure subscriptions into logical containers. This allows administrators to apply RBAC permissions and Azure Policies across multiple subscriptions at once.

I created a management group named:

az104-mg1

During this task, I learned that:

Management Groups simplify administration in enterprise environments
Permissions and policies inherit through the hierarchy
The Root Management Group contains all subscriptions within the Azure tenant
Management Groups improve centralized governance

This feature is especially useful for organizations managing multiple subscriptions across departments or regions.

Task 2: Review and Assign a Built-In Azure Role

Next, I explored Azure built-in RBAC roles through the Access Control (IAM) section.

Azure includes many predefined roles such as:

Owner
Contributor
Reader
Virtual Machine Contributor

I assigned the:

Virtual Machine Contributor

role to the Help Desk group.

This role allows users to:

Create and manage virtual machines

but restricts them from:

Accessing the operating system
Managing storage accounts
Managing virtual networking resources

This task demonstrated how Azure RBAC helps administrators securely delegate responsibilities without granting excessive permissions.

Key Concepts Learned
Azure RBAC controls authorization within Azure
Built-in roles simplify access management
Permissions should follow least privilege principles
Group-based assignments are easier to manage than individual permissions
Task 3: Create a Custom RBAC Role

In this task, I created a custom RBAC role to provide more granular permissions.

I cloned the:

Support Request Contributor

role and modified its permissions.

The custom role created was:

Custom Support Request

I removed unnecessary permissions related to:

Microsoft.Support

provider registration by adding them to the NotActions section.

This demonstrated how custom roles use JSON configurations to define:

Actions
NotActions
AssignableScopes
Key Concepts Learned
Custom roles provide precise permission control
Built-in roles may grant too many permissions
Least privilege is critical for cloud security
Azure RBAC roles are configured using JSON
Task 4: Monitor Role Assignments with Activity Logs

The final task focused on monitoring RBAC activity using Azure Activity Logs.

The Activity Log allows administrators to:

Monitor role assignments
Track permission changes
Audit administrative actions
Investigate suspicious behavior

I reviewed RBAC-related events associated with the management group and explored filtering options within the logs.

Key Concepts Learned
Activity Logs improve visibility into Azure administration
RBAC changes should be audited regularly
Monitoring improves security and compliance
Skills Learned
Managing Azure subscriptions
Configuring Management Groups
Assigning built-in Azure RBAC roles
Creating custom RBAC roles
Applying least privilege access
Monitoring Azure Activity Logs
Understanding Azure governance and access control
Conclusion

This lab provided practical experience with Azure governance and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).

I learned how to organize subscriptions using Management Groups, securely assign permissions using built-in and custom roles, and monitor administrative changes through Activity Logs.

These skills are essential for Azure administrators and cloud security professionals because identity and access management are foundational components of securing Azure environments.
